6.9.text:
Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:

a. humanity, including respect for the rights of women and children, protection and security for victims, best, affordable and fast service, sensitivity and empathy, maintaining victim confidentiality;
b. justice, including equality, legal certainty, presumption of innocence, equality before the law, best interests of victims, not blaming victims;
c. transparency, accountability, and participatory;
d. tolerance and non-discrimination in differences of race, between groups, gender, religion, ethnicity, age, economic background and social status.

Article 3

(1) The purpose of implementing protection of women and children victims of violence is to:

a. prevent the occurrence of all forms of violence against women and children;
b. provide initial handling of victims;
c. provide health services for victims both physical and psychological; d. provide guarantees of protection, security, and legal certainty to women and children victims of violence, witnesses, and reporters; e. seek victim recovery and rehabilitation;
f. empower victims and reintegration;
g. conduct coordination and evaluation of handling violence against women and children.
(2) Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect; and
e. restriction of public access.

Article 5

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 4, also receive special rights, as follows: a. the right to obtain respect and services for survival, growth and development;

b. the right to basic services;

c. the right to be free from negative stigma; and

d. the right to obtain freedom to choose and decide on self-management after parental divorce.

Article 10

(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, counseling and/or guidance.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes efforts as follows: a. increase the quantity and quality of education both formal, non-formal and informal;

b. increase accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income improvement, and social services; c. open employment opportunities for women;

d. build community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence; e. build and provide a complete and easily accessible information system;

f. build networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations engaged in and/or concerned with women and children; and

g. open complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence based at the sub-district to village level.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Article 4

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:

a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings; b. the right to prompt complaint handling and fast and affordable service. c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
h. the right to assistance;
i. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims; and
j. the right to rehabilitation and social reintegration.

Article 19

(1) The Regional Government is obligated to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from violence. (2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;

b. technical guidance;

c. education and training;

d. provision of facilities;

e. monitoring; and

f. evaluation.

(3) In order to realize the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence, guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with minimum service standards.

Article 17

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government cooperates with: a. the government;

b. the provincial government;

c. other Regional Governments; and

d. non-governmental institutions.

(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:

a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
d. referral mechanisms for case follow-up in accordance with applicable legislation.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) is set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

